# Terraform
**/.terraform/*
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc

# Secret-bearing variable files (non-secret terraform.tfvars is committed)
*.secret.tfvars
secrets.auto.tfvars

# Local shell env (e.g. direnv's .envrc holding HCLOUD_TOKEN — Read Only
# only, per AGENTS.md; never the Read & Write token)
.envrc
.env

# OS/editor cruft
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Working trees. AGENTS.md requires every change to take one — at
# `.worktrees/<name>` generally, or `.claude/worktrees/<name>` under the Claude
# Code binding — and each is a full copy of the repository living inside it.
# Untracked and unignored, they showed up in `git status` and stood one
# `git add -A` from the main working tree away from being committed wholesale.
#
# This does NOT keep them out of the CI-configuration suite's walks: a
# filesystem walk does not consult git. That is `walked_files()`'s prune list,
# and `terraform_lockfile_directories()` explains why the two are separate
# fixes. Only `.claude/worktrees/` is ignored, not `.claude/`, whose
# `commands/` and `skills/` are committed.
.worktrees/
.claude/worktrees/

# The per-working-tree ANSIBLE_HOME that ansible/scripts/run-molecule points
# Molecule at, holding this tree's own ephemeral directories. Machine state, not
# repository content — and it must not be shared, which is the whole point.
.molecule-home/

# Ansible: Galaxy-installed role content, pinned by exact version in that
# file, not committed itself. Fetched via:
#   ansible-galaxy role install -r ansible/requirements.yml -p ansible/roles
ansible/roles/geerlingguy.docker/

# Python bytecode from the CI-configuration test suite (.github/tests/).
__pycache__/

# npm's install tree for .github/package.json, which pins the OpenSpec CLI that
# pr-validation.yml validates the specification record with. The manifest and
# its lockfile ARE committed -- they are the pin; what is installed from them
# is not. `npm ci` recreates this from the lockfile exactly.
node_modules/

# Private keys. None is ever committed, and nothing else here says so.
#
# The hazard is specific rather than general: docs/bootstrap-a-new-host.md
# stage 0.3 generates six passphrase-less keypairs, stage 4.2 runs `git add -A`
# from this directory, and those two steps are most of a working day apart.
# gitleaks catches the content at commit time -- but it is a pre-commit hook,
# and stage 0 runs before `pre-commit install` necessarily has. This is the
# layer that does not depend on having installed anything.
#
# Conventional names, anywhere in the tree:
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

# And this repository's own scheme, which produces names with NO extension --
# <company>-ops, <company>-root, <company>-platform, <company>-platform-staging,
# <company>-ansible-ci-<stack>, <company>-<app>-deploy -- so no suffix pattern
# reaches them. §0.3 writes each into ~/.ssh/; these catch the relative-path
# slip that writes one here instead.
#
# ANCHORED TO THE ROOT with a leading slash, deliberately. Unanchored, `*-ops`
# would silently swallow a directory called `commerce-ops` added later, which
# is the same class of accident this block exists to prevent. The slip lands in
# the directory the command is run from, and that is this one.
/*-ops
/*-root
/*-platform
/*-platform-staging
/*-ansible-ci-*
/*-deploy

# Public halves are not secret, and one being hidden would be its own defect.
!*.pub
